{"id":"19.02.BacktoTheHome","contentHtml":"<p>This project is a turning point in my career, my first <strong>Global Game Jam</strong> (theme \"What home means to you\"). The game was based on a <strong>concept I proposed and then refined with the team</strong>. This initiative naturally led to me taking on the role of <strong>leader and lead developer</strong> in a team of 5 people, implementing the lion's share of the project.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>Challenge</h2>\n<p>The initial task was to create a winning game in 48 hours on the GGJ theme \"What home means to you\". After our triumph, the task transformed into a <strong>strategic one</strong>: <mark>quickly port the <code>Unity</code> prototype to a completely different engine and programming language</mark> to meet the business requirements of a potential publisher.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>Engineering and Leadership Solution</h2>\n<p>The project went through two key stages, at each of which I made key decisions.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Game Jam Stage (Unity):</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Developed original core mechanics: a bus <strong>autonomously circles</strong> a modular \"island\", and the player's task is to \"shoot\" passengers in time so they hit the houses. Once a house is filled, it <mark>transforms into a rocket and takes off</mark>, after which the bus moves to this vacated island, continuing the game loop.</li>\n<li>I was responsible for implementing <strong>all core mechanics, creating 3D assets</strong> in <code>Blender</code>, and coordinating the team's work.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Publisher Pitch Stage (Port to PlayCanvas):</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>After winning the jam, <strong>we were invited to the office of a large company, where we conducted a live demonstration of the game for management and employees</strong>.</li>\n<li>Realizing that the publisher was focused on the Facebook games market, I made a <strong>strategic decision</strong> to move the project. <code>Unity WebGL</code> at that time was bulky, and <code>PlayCanvas</code> (<code>JavaScript</code>) was ideal for their ecosystem.</li>\n<li>Despite the fact that most of the team dropped out, I <mark>independently rewrote the entire project in JavaScript</mark>, bringing it to the state of a product ready for demonstration.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<hr>\n<h2>Outcome</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Triumph at Game Jam:</strong> Our team <mark>won the first two nominations</mark> — <strong>\"Best Idea\"</strong> and <strong>\"Best Game according to TapClap\"</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Technical Flexibility:</strong> The project proved the ability to quickly master a new technology (<code>PlayCanvas</code>) and adapt the product to business requirements.</li>\n<li><strong>Invaluable Lesson:</strong> Despite the failure with the deal, this experience became my key lesson in team management, interaction with business, and the <strong>importance of strategic technology choice</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n","published":true,"title":"Back to The Home","date":"2019-02","type":"project","tagsArray":["PC","Web","Hackathon","Unity","C#","Playcanvas","JS","Blender","VFX"],"heroVideo":["laUt-sk0Tf8"],"shortDescription":"<p>Led the team at GGJ and won two awards. Strategic challenge: <mark>porting the entire prototype to another engine</mark> to pitch to a publisher.</p>\n","storeLinks":["https://v3.globalgamejam.org/2019/games/back-home-12"],"role":"jam","projectIcon":"/images/projects/19.02.BacktoTheHome/icon.webp","cardBackground":"/images/projects/19.02.BacktoTheHome/card.mp4?v=1765047012069","screenshots":[{"src":"/images/projects/19.02.BacktoTheHome/screenshots/1.webp","width":1080,"height":809},{"src":"/images/projects/19.02.BacktoTheHome/screenshots/2.webp","width":921,"height":1567},{"src":"/images/projects/19.02.BacktoTheHome/screenshots/3.webp","width":1080,"height":606},{"src":"/images/projects/19.02.BacktoTheHome/screenshots/4.webp","width":785,"height":1080},{"src":"/images/projects/19.02.BacktoTheHome/screenshots/5.webp","widt